steps.body:
**Blocks qa's back-office runs** (payment, account, news, contact-send, edit-order, orders-api cleanup, maintenance): staging answers 404 to them until this is set. Press **Generate**, then:
- set it as `E2E_STAGING_TOKEN` on Netlify's staging context (the env push does it);
- save it in `.env.e2e-staging` (qa never uses `.env.staging` for it);
- save it in the password manager, and as the GitHub repository secret of the same name, if the workflow needs it.

It is staging only; production must not hold the name. The suite no longer needs a Netlify token (#266).

**The checkout lane needs the staging token too.** Its live checks for the rate-limit bypass (#164 item 4) and the hand run of AO-17 (#160) cannot run without it: `.env.staging` must hold `STAGING_TOKEN=<value>` and `.env.e2e-staging` must hold `E2E_STAGING_TOKEN=<value>`. Both files are ignored by git. Never paste a value into a chat.

When you're ready, any order:
- error tracking parts C–F (#259–#262), including the Checkly trial check;
- the remaining account strings (#158), the photo-ask plan (#199) and /about/'s people section;
- the automated accessibility scan (QF1, #197): no CI gate and no rescan before launch, and the launch bugs already on #159 still ship as planned;
- a function that filters for production deploy failures and pages your phone through the alerts check (D-NOTIFY, #248);
- the header vector file (D15, #170), a manual keyboard and VoiceOver pass, and #181 and #184's decisions;
- Resend's Contacts and Segments (D-148, #148): built and held on `hold/148`; the old API still works, so launch day has no migration step. **Switch maintenance mode on first, for the cutover** (`bin/nff --production maintenance on`, then off when it is checked), so no sign-up or order lands half in the old audience and half in the new;
- nothing to check in November about the drop site's "starting October 17" (W10, #218): the launch email drops the date itself from Oct 17, and the dated September post keeps it on purpose;
- delete the stray notes in the repo root (#159);
- move both repositories (`north-foster-farm`, `nff-dashboard`) to your personal account and make them private. They are public today, which is why Netlify's free plan can host an organization repository. Plan it as one item:
  - **GitHub Pro** (about $4 a month) is needed for branch protection and rulesets on private personal repositories, and for 3,000 Actions minutes;
  - **Actions minutes:** staging's jobs every 15 minutes are about 2,900 runs a month, plus CI. Proposed: hourly staging jobs, or move them off Actions;
  - **Anything that reads a repository without signing in breaks once it is private:** this guide's `raw.githubusercontent.com` fetch (moot if the dashboard version has replaced it) and image links in issues;
  - **Re-link:** the Netlify sites, the GitHub apps (Netlify, Claude), the coordinator's and lanes' access, webhooks and the `STAGING_TOKEN` repository secret;
  - **Order:** transfer, check redirects, make private, then re-link and check the deploys.
- script the revert of a release as `bin/nff rollback revert` (branch, revert range, CI dispatch, watch, fast-forward, push). It stops on any failure and asks for your go before it pushes `main`;
- the checkout lane's `Record-shape:` commit-trailer check in `lint-and-test` (`docs/rollback.md`), and the check that the rotation log has a seed line for every secret (`docs/secrets.md`).

Tick this when you've told Claude your order.
- **Evaluate a scoped Square OAuth login** in place of the long-lived access token (your 10/18 note on #267). A research item, not built.
- a switch that stops only the PayPal and Venmo path, so a leaked PayPal secret does not need the whole shop closed while Card keeps taking orders (`docs/secrets.md`, PayPal);
- evaluate a scoped Square login (OAuth with the minimum permissions) instead of the all-powerful personal access token;
- replace bin/nff's Blobs calls with the site's own admin API, behind an admin session or a scoped admin key, so the Netlify token can be deleted.
